{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg-2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**The Last Watch at Gannet Rock**\n\nFor thirty-one years, Elspeth Morrow climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Gannet Rock light every evening at dusk, and she had never once missed a night.\n\nThe station had been automated in 1987. The brass clockwork had been replaced by a solar-powered lamp that needed no one, and the Coast Guard sent a letter every spring reminding her that her position was, technically, no longer required. She kept the letter in the drawer with the logbooks. She kept climbing.\n\nHer husband, Tom, had been the keeper before her. He had taught her the rhythms of the light: the four-second flash, the eight-second dark, the way the lens turned on its bath of mercury like a slow, heavy thought. When he died of a stroke in the cold February of her fifty-second year, the island seemed to lean a little further into the sea, and she half expected the tower to follow.\n\nIt did not. She climbed the steps the next evening, and the evening after that.\n\nHer routine was simple. She polished the lens glass each morning with a chamois and vinegar. She wound the old backup mechanism every Sunday, though it had not been needed since the switch. She wrote in the logbook: *Wind NW, 15 knots. Visibility good. Light operating. Two gulls on the western rail.* Sometimes she added a line that had nothing to do with weather. *Tom would have liked the sunset.* *The seals are back.* *I dreamed about the stairs.*\n\nThe mainland ferry passed twice a week, and the young deckhand, a boy named Caleb with a sunburned nose, always waved. One afternoon in October he rowed a small skiff to the landing with a box of groceries and an envelope.\n\n\"From the district office,\" he said, squinting up at her. \"They want you to sign something. Says it's the final paperwork.\"\n\nShe took the envelope and read it on the rocks while the wind pulled at her cardigan. It was a decommissioning notice. The light would go fully dark in December. The tower would be sealed, and the island would revert to the gulls and the seals.\n\nCaleb shifted from foot to foot. \"Sorry, ma'am. I didn't write it.\"\n\n\"I know you didn't, love.\" She folded the paper carefully. \"Thank you for bringing it.\"\n\nThat evening she climbed the one hundred and twelve steps for the last time that season. She reached the gallery, her breath short and her knees complaining, and stood in the rising wind. Below, the Atlantic moved in long grey ridges. Far to the east, the mainland lights were blinking on, scattered and uncertain.\n\nShe went inside and switched on the lamp. Its beam swept out across the water, steady and unhurried, reaching for ships that might not come, for fishermen who had long since found their way home by other means.\n\nShe took out the logbook and wrote: *Wind SW, 10 knots. Visibility excellent. Light operating.*\n\nThen, after a long pause, she added: *Still here.*\n\nShe closed the book, pulled her cardigan tight, and sat in Tom's old chair to watch the light go round."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":957,"cache_read_input_tokens":4215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":957,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu-1","agent_id":"tid-1","session_id":"sess-1","uuid":"uuid-47","timestamp":"2026-11-24T01:38:27.241Z","request_id":"apireq-2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write lighthouse keeper story"}
