ng. Says it's the final paperwork.\"\n\nShe took the envelope and read it on the rocks while the wind pulled at her cardigan. It was a decommissioning notice. The light would go fully dark in December. The tower would be sealed, and the island would revert to the gulls and the seals.\n\nCaleb shifted from foot to foot. \"Sorry, ma'am. I didn't write it.\"\n\n\"I know you didn't, love.\" She folded the paper carefully. \"Thank you for bringing it.\"\n\nThat evening she climbed the one hundred and twelve steps for the last time that season. She reached the gallery, her breath short and her knees complaining, and stood in the rising wind. Below, the Atlantic moved in long grey ridges. Far to the east, the mainland lights were blinking on, scattered and uncertain.\n\nShe went inside and switched on the lamp. Its beam swept out across the water, steady and unhurried, reaching for ships that might not come, for fishermen who had long since found their way home by other means.\n\nShe took out the logbook and wrote: *Wind SW, 10 knots. Visibility excellent. Light operating.*\n\nThen, after a long pause, she added: *Still here.*\n\nShe closed the book, pulled her cardigan tight, and sat in Tom's old chair to watch the light go round.","usage":{"total_tokens":6244,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":7148},"uuid":"uuid-49","session_id":"sess-1"}
